Mr. Speaker, I rise to wish a very special 100th birthday to my friend and constituent, Mr. Oakley Ferris. Mr. Ferris grew up near Lexington, Kentucky. After graduating from high school, he enlisted in the United States Army, and fought heroically in World War II, after returning from his tour of duty in Europe, Mr. Ferris settled in Covington, Kentucky, and began working as a salesman, traveling up and down the east coast. It was on a sales trip to Miami that Mr. Ferris met his future wife, Eva. An immigrant from Havana, Cuba. Eva went on to study at Columbia University. Together, the Ferrises built a very successful business. Mr. Ferris and his late wife made countless charitable investments in our community. I'm grateful for all of that that Mr. Ferris has done for my constituents. And I'm honored to represent him in Washington, D.C. Happy birthday, Oakley. May you have many more years of good health and happiness. I yield back.
